Explanation: The tasks are sorted based on (minimum required energy - actual energy used) so that tasks needing a larger extra energy buffer are completed first.
We maintain a variable ans representing the minimum initial energy required so far.
For each task, we update ans using Math.max(ans + actual, minimum) to ensure we have enough energy to both start and complete the task.
This greedy approach guarantees the minimum initial energy needed to finish all tasks efficiently.
The overall time complexity is O(n log n) due to sorting.

Code: 
class Solution {
    public int minimumEffort(int[][] tasks) {
        Arrays.sort(tasks,(a,b)-> a[1]-a[0]-(b[1]-b[0]));
        int ans=0;
        for(int[] task: tasks){
            ans=Math.max(ans+ task[0],task[1]);
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
